<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/waves.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/waves-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/waves.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/claim-appraisals/">Appraisals</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Hurricane &amp; Nor’easter Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Hurricane Claim Appraisal for Long Island, NYC and the Coast</h1><p class="lede">Hurricane claim appraisal on the New York coast often hinges on what the wind did versus what the water did. We separate the two carefully and value the wind-driven portion your homeowners policy actually covers.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Regions</dt><dd>Long Island, NYC, Westchester coast</dd></div><div><dt>Key split</dt><dd>Wind vs. storm surge</dd></div><div><dt>Deductibles</dt><dd>Often 1%–5% of dwelling</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>A long coastal storm record</h2>
<p>The 1938 Long Island Express, Tropical Storm Irene in 2011, Superstorm Sandy on October 29, 2012 and Tropical Storm Isaias in August 2020 all left their mark on coastal New York. Nor’easters add a steady winter drumbeat of wind and tide. Any <strong>hurricane claim appraisal</strong> here has to deal with the same core problem: separating covered wind damage from excluded flood.</p>
<h2>Wind versus surge</h2>
<p>Homeowners policies generally exclude flood, including storm surge. Flood is usually covered, if at all, by an NFIP or private flood policy. Sandy showed how messy that split can be on Staten Island, in the Rockaways and in Long Beach, where homes took wind and water in the same night. We document the wind evidence that stands on its own:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof and siding damage above any water line</li>
<li>Interior water staining from openings rather than rising water</li>
<li>Tree fall and debris impact consistent with wind direction</li>
<li>Window, door and skylight failures</li>
</ul>
<p>Timing matters too. After a major storm, adjusters may inspect weeks later, after debris has been cleared and water lines have faded. Your own photos and videos from the first days, contractor notes, and any tarping or board-up invoices become the best record of what the wind did. We organise that evidence by elevation and by room so the other appraiser and, if needed, the umpire can follow it without guesswork. Where the same house also has a flood claim, we keep the two scopes clearly separated to avoid any suggestion of double recovery.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Flood policies are different</b>NFIP policies have their own appraisal provision, limited to certain disputes, and strict proof-of-loss rules. Our <a href="/policyholder-guides/basement-water-sewer-backup-vs-flood/">basement water, sewer backup or flood guide</a> explains the differences; talk to a New York attorney about any flood claim deadline.</div>
<h2>Percentage hurricane and windstorm deductibles</h2>
<p>Many policies on Long Island, in New York City and along the Westchester shoreline carry a hurricane or windstorm deductible, generally 1% to 5% of dwelling coverage, that applies only when the storm meets the policy’s definition. Whether that trigger was met is a policy question, but the size of the claim relative to the deductible is what makes appraisal worthwhile. Try our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> and read the <a href="/policyholder-guides/hurricane-deductibles-long-island-nyc/">hurricane deductibles guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Nor’easters and winter coastal storms</h2>
<p>Nor’easters rarely trigger hurricane deductibles but can do serious damage to roofs, siding and bulkhead-adjacent structures, often combined with snow and ice. Repeated storms can also blur the line between old and new damage, so dated photos after each event help. We value those losses under the standard deductible unless your policy says otherwise.</p>
<h2>Serving the coast</h2>
<p>We work in <a href="/where-we-appraise/hempstead/">Hempstead</a>, Islip, Staten Island, southern Brooklyn and Queens, and the Westchester Sound shore, where capes, bungalows and raised homes each present their own repair challenges.</p>

<h2>Questions about hurricane &amp; nor’easter appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Will my homeowners policy pay for storm surge damage?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally no. Storm surge is treated as flood, which homeowners policies exclude; it is usually covered only by a separate flood policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>When does a hurricane deductible apply in New York?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Only when the event meets the definition in your policy, which varies by insurer. Check the declarations page and the deductible endorsement.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide how much damage was wind and how much was water?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Panels sometimes allocate damage between causes as part of valuing the loss, but whether flood damage is covered is a coverage question outside appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
